<div class="main-text" style="text-align: justify;">
    <p><b><i><h1>Raspberry</h1></b><br>

    Raspberries are a popular and productive fruit crop in the UK, thriving in the **cool and temperate climate**. They can be grown successfully 
    across most of the country, including <strong>Scotland</strong>, where they are widely cultivated. With proper care, raspberries can produce an abundance 
    of fruit over the summer and autumn months.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Best Growing Conditions</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Climate:</strong> Raspberries grow well in the UK’s mild summers and cool winters.<br>
    - <strong>Soil:</strong> Well-drained, fertile soil with a pH between 5.5 and 6.5 is ideal.<br>
    - <strong>Sunlight:</strong> Full sun is best, but they can tolerate partial shade.<br>
    - <strong>Watering:</strong> Consistent moisture is important, especially during fruiting, but avoid waterlogging.<br>
    - <strong>Support:</strong> Most varieties need support, such as canes or trellises, to keep the fruit off the ground.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Common Raspberry Diseases</h2></b><br>
    The current database does not contain any records of raspberry-related diseases.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Tips for Growing Raspberries in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Choose the right variety:</strong> ‘Glen Ample’, ‘Autumn Bliss’, and ‘Malling Jewel’ are excellent choices for UK growers.<br>
    - <strong>Pruning:</strong> Summer-fruiting varieties should be pruned after harvest, while autumn-fruiting raspberries should be cut back in late winter.<br>
    - <strong>Mulching:</strong> Apply organic mulch, such as compost or bark, to retain moisture and suppress weeds.<br>
    - <strong>Spacing:</strong> Space plants about 45 cm apart in rows to allow for good air circulation and ease of harvesting.<br>
    - <strong>Bird Protection:</strong> Birds love raspberries, so using netting can help protect your crop.<br><br>

    Raspberries are an easy-to-grow and rewarding fruit, making them a great choice for UK gardens and allotments.
    </i></p>
